%>
%>Mike Piff asked about modifying letter.cls with classes and packages.
%>
%>I asked a similar question soon after LaTeX2e was released. One solution
%>(which I use) is to define a new class somas which calls the letter class.
%>somas can modify the letter class and can define new class options, and
%>inherit the class options (eg a4paper) provided by letter. (using the
%>\PassOptionsToClass command.)
%>
%>There's no reason why mjplet could not be a class which calls
%>somas which calls letter. (except that you might run out of memory).
%>
%>Alternatively, mjplet could be a package which modifies the behaviour of somas
%>by defining \signature etc. I guess this is preferable, and is akin to the
%>`style option' of LaTeX2.09
%>
%>Dean Ayres
%>

I only quote this to point out that I am not alone in not understanding the
distinction between option, package and class. Why this obfuscation?

Anyway, the list environment is presumably a package to do lists, the output
routine is a package to do output, so everything is up for grabs:-)

However, I do agree that the \RequiresPackage command is *extremely*
important if you take this attitude, equivalent to Modula-2's IMPORT
statement. Are we also to be treated to DEFINITION and IMPLEMENTATION
modules in LaTeX-3?:->

Mike Piff

